The idea is to split array into smaller components, sort them then merge them.
Worst case time is O(n Lg(n)). Lg n is the split part n being the merge part.

The operative idea being:
-> sorting smaller arrays are faster
-> merging sorted arrays is O(n) not O(n^2) (like it is with insertion sort)

=================================================
	RECURSIVE
=================================================
void mergesort(A, low, high) {
     if (low >= high) return;

     mid = (low + high) / 2;
     async {
          mergesort(A, low, mid);
	  mergesort(A, mid + 1, high);
     }
     merge(A, low, mid, high);
}

void merge(A, low, mid, high) {
     left  = low;
     right = mid + 1;
     size  = high - low + 1;

     for (i = 0; i < size; i++) {
     	 if (A[left] < A[right]) {
	    new[i] = A[left++];
	 } else if (A[left] > A[right]) {
	    new[i] = A[right++];
	 } else {
	    new[i] = A[left++];
	    right++;
	 }
     }     
}


=================================================
	Iterative
=================================================
The idea is to form a sorting network. We need to work more to extend our
existing Messaging Libraries ParallelMessage to better support Parallel
Networks but for now this should do. This has the drawback that it 
lock steps over each row of the network whereas in reality thats not 
strictly true.

void mergesort(A, low, high) {
     totalelements = high - low + 1;
     totalrows = logBase2(RoundUpPowerOf2(totalelements));

     for (rownum = 0; rownum < totalrows; rownum++) {
     	 stride = 1 << (rownum + 1);

	 parallel_for(colnum = 0; < totalelements / stride; colnum++) {
	 	start = colnum * stride;
		merge(A, start, (start + stride) / 2, start + stride - 1);
	 }

     }
}

